[0.010, "o", "\r\n==============================================================================\r\nQ: Why did site sessions collapse in August?\r\n==============================================================================\r\n\r\nConfidence: insufficient evidence\r\nTook 87s over 5 steps, 26,574 tokens, 15 observations gathered, 12 cited\r\n\r\nANSWER\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  Site sessions did not collapse in August: GA4's own series simply stops recording after 2026-08-03, and PostHog pageview data for the same window shows flat traffic with no level shift through 2026-08-15.  [ga4.get_sessions 6a9d973e, posthog.event_trend 7b40bbeb]\r\n  On the three August days GA4 does report, sessions were higher (324-333/day) than the prior July rate (~150-166/day), the opposite of a collapse.  [ga4.get_sessions 6a9d973e, ga4.compare_periods d4b66669]\r\n\r\nFINDINGS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  GA4 sessions series stops on 2026-08-03, not b"]
[0.000, "o", "ecause sessions fell  (high)\r\n    - The GA4 sessions series for the requested Aug 1–15 window has data only through 2026-08-03; the remaining 12 days have no rows at all, which the tool itself flags as a gap rather than a low value.  [ga4.get_sessions 6a9d973e]\r\n    - For the days GA4 does report in August (Aug 1–3), daily sessions were 333, 324 and 327 — higher than the ~150-166/day seen through mid-to-late July, not lower.  [ga4.get_sessions 6a9d973e, ga4.compare_periods d4b66669]\r\n  PostHog pageviews show no corresponding movement in August  (high)\r\n    - PostHog $pageview daily counts from 2026-07-16 through 2026-08-15 show no sustained level shift; the automated check found day-to-day variation within noise across the full 31-day window.  [posthog.event_trend 7b40bbeb]\r\n  No commit, deploy, or Slack record  (medium)\r\n    - No commits were found in acme/company-website, acme/web, or acme/product since 2026-07-15, so no deploy in the visible commit history coincides with the GA4 gap.  [github.commits"]
[0.000, "o", " f5edb293, github.commits 27f2aee6, github.commits 2a58cc25]\r\n    - No Slack messages or annotations mention a traffic drop, a GA4 property change, or an analytics tag change, and no decision record on analytics tracking was found.  [slack.search_messages 91c5141b, slack.search_messages 723cc8ff, slack.search_messages 1b019852, slack.find_decision 36069262, posthog.annotations 6c36fbe8]\r\n\r\nCHARTS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  $pageview over time\r\n  value\r\n    value              |▃█▅▂▆▄██▅▆▆▇█▁▄▅▃▅▂▇▅▂▆▃▂▄▂▂▇▆▁|  1,323 → 1,574\r\n                        2026-07-16 … 2026-08-15\r\n\r\n  sessions over time\r\n  sessions\r\n    sessions           |▁▁▁▂▁▁▁▁▁▂▁▁▁▁▂▁███|  150 → 333\r\n                        2026-07-16 … 2026-08-03\r\n\r\n\r\nHYPOTHESES TESTED\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  [contradicted] Site sessions genui"]
[0.000, "o", "nely collapsed in August due to a product or marketing change.\r\n      The days GA4 does report in August show higher sessions than July, and PostHog pageviews (an independent measurement) show no level shift at all across the same window. There is no real traffic collapse to explain.\r\n  [inconclusive] The GA4 sessions collection stopped or broke after 2026-08-03 (a data/tracking incident), which is what produces the appearance of a collapse when the full 31-day period is requested.\r\n      The series_gap_note and data_trust fields explicitly flag that the series has no data after 2026-08-03 though the range runs to 2026-08-15, and warns not to treat this as a decline without establishing which it is. Since PostHog shows flat traffic through the same window, an emitter-side collection stoppage is the more consistent explanation than a real drop.\r\n  [inconclusive] A code deploy to the website, product app, or mobile app caused the apparent GA4 gap by removing or breaking the tracking tag.\r\n      No commits were "]
[0.000, "o", "returned for company-website, web, or product repos since 2026-07-15, so no deploy artefact could be identified as a cause. This does not confirm no deploy happened — commit history may not be visible for this window — only that none was found to cite.\r\n\r\nRECOMMENDATIONS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  P1 Check the GA4 property's measurement tag / data stream status directly in GA4 admin for a stoppage starting 2026-08-04, since this report's tools cannot see emitter-side configuration.\r\n      why: The gap begins exactly where the series stops (2026-08-03), and no code or Slack evidence explains it, pointing to a tracking-layer issue rather than a real traffic change.\r\n      [ga4.get_sessions 6a9d973e]\r\n  P2 Use PostHog $pageview data as the traffic source of truth until GA4 collection is confirmed restored.\r\n      why: PostHog shows no interruption and no level shift through 2026-08-15, making it the more reliable series for this window.\r\n      [posthog."]
[0.000, "o", "event_trend 7b40bbeb]\r\n\r\nRISKS AND CAVEATS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - The GA4 commit and Slack searches returning empty results are consistent with no relevant event occurring, but equally consistent with those events existing outside what these tools surfaced (e.g., a deploy to an unlisted repo, or a Slack channel/keyword not matched by the queries tried). This report cannot rule out an untracked cause of the GA4 gap.\r\n  - Only 3 days of August GA4 data exist, so the claim that August sessions were higher than July is based on a very small sample and could reverse in later days that this series cannot show.\r\n  - 3 claim(s) go beyond their evidence and are retained with reduced confidence rather than removed, because the rest of each one holds. What the check found:\r\n- executive_summary[2]: asked as an open question, with this claim withheld, the same evidence does not establish a cause: All queries returned zero results: no commits since 2026-07-15 in"]
[0.000, "o", " the checked rep\r\n- findings[0].claims[2]: asked as an open question, with this claim withheld, the same evidence does not establish a cause: The comparison returned zero rows and unequal data coverage (current period only \r\n- findings[1].claims[1]: asked as an open question, with this claim withheld, the same evidence does not establish a cause: GA4 sessions actually rose (from ~155-165/day to ~325-330/day) on 2026-08-01 thro\r\n  - 4 causal claim(s) were withheld: a separate check of the evidence gathered, made without seeing this report, found that it does not support a definitive answer about cause. The GA4 series shows a hard cessation at 2026-08-03 rather than a gradual decline, and the tool's own notes flag this as indistinguishable between 'traffic stopped' and 'measurement stopped'; meanwhile PostHog pageviews over the same period show no level shift at all, suggesting the GA4 gap is instrumentation failure, not a real traffic collapse. All searches for a deploy, decision, or announcement returned zero"]
[0.000, "o", " rows, so nothing here dates or names a cause compatible with the onset. What would settle it: A PostHog $pageview series or other independent traffic source covering the same Aug 3-15+ window to check whether real traffic also stopped (posthog trend here shows no shift, contradicting the GA4 gap); GA4 property/tag configuration or admin change history to see if the measurement tag was removed or the property\r\n\r\nDATA QUALITY\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - GA4 sessions series is missing all data from 2026-08-04 through 2026-08-15 (12 of 15 requested days in August); the tool's own data_trust check marked this 'degraded' and flagged the gap explicitly.\r\n  - The July vs August GA4 comparison_periods call could not produce a valid percent change because the current period only had 3 days of data against 31 requested; the tool marked the windows non-comparable.\r\n  - 9 of the 12 cited observation(s) returned no results (ga4.compare_periods, github.commits, posth"]
[0.000, "o", "og.annotations, slack.find_decision, slack.search_messages). Conclusions resting on them describe what was not found, which is not the same as what does not exist.\r\n\r\nSOURCES\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  bce1f88e  ga4.compare_periods  eval://ga4__compare_periods\r\n  d4b66669  ga4.compare_periods  eval://ga4__compare_periods\r\n  6a9d973e  ga4.get_sessions  eval://ga4__get_sessions\r\n  f5edb293  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  27f2aee6  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  2a58cc25  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  6c36fbe8  posthog.annotations  eval://posthog__annotations\r\n  7b40bbeb  posthog.event_trend  eval://posthog__event_trend\r\n  36069262  slack.find_decision  eval://slack__find_decision\r\n  91c5141b  slack.search_messages  eval://slack__search_messages\r\n  723cc8ff  slack.search_messages  eval://slack__search_messages\r\n  1b019852  slack.search_messages  eval://slack__search_messages\r\n"]
